# edge/Caddyfile
#
# One site block per exposed port. Every block below shares the same
# hostname (NOIP_HOSTNAME), so Caddy obtains and renews a SINGLE
# certificate for that hostname and reuses it across every port — you are
# not paying an extra cert per service.
#
# To add a new service:
#   1. Copy a block below, change the port and the backend.
#   2. Add the matching port to edge/docker-compose.yaml's `caddy.ports`.
#   3. Forward that port on your router to Ocra.
#   4. docker compose up -d --force-recreate caddy
#
# Backends are addressed by Tailscale IP, not LAN IP or hostname — same
# reasoning as everywhere else in this repo (see monitoring/prometheus/
# prometheus.yml's header comment): LAN IPs drift, and MagicDNS has been
# unreliable from Ocra specifically.

{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

# ── Nextcloud (Aery) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Routed through wake-proxy (monitoring/wake-proxy/), NOT directly to
# Aery, so public visitors get the same auto-wake-on-demand + holding
# page behavior that internal/Tailscale clients already get. wake-proxy
# also rewrites the Host header to Aery's own trusted hostname:port, so
# the only thing needed on the Nextcloud side is adding this No-IP
# hostname to trusted_domains — see README.md for the exact config.
{$NOIP_HOSTNAME}:8443 {
	reverse_proxy 100.107.52.17:8093
}

# ── TEMP diagnostic block ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Grafana, reached over Ocra's own LAN IP (not Tailscale, not wake-proxy)
# — purely to test whether the public path (router → Ocra → Caddy)
# works at all, independent of Tailscale/Funnel. Remove this once the
# diagnosis is done; Grafana has its own login so it's safe to leave
# briefly exposed, but it's not meant to stay here long-term (see
# README.md's Security section).
{$NOIP_HOSTNAME}:8444 {
	reverse_proxy 192.168.68.107:3000
}

# ── Template for the next service ──────────────────────────────────
# Ports 8444-8448 are already forwarded on the router (see
# edge/README.md and docker-compose.yaml's `caddy.ports`) as a reserved
# range, so adding a service here is the ONLY step left -- no router
# trip needed. Uncomment, pick the next free port, fill in the backend.
#
# {$NOIP_HOSTNAME}:8445 {
# 	reverse_proxy <backend-tailscale-ip>:<backend-port>
# }
